title: A sops-written .inf in an unencrypted slot fails File::SOPS MAC verification
updated: 2026-08-21T04:43:47Z
---

Found by the format lane while measuring karr #102 (sops 3.13.3 at /tmp/sops).
Pre-existing, independent of #102, and NOT fixed by #102's candidate change.

ADR 0013 records the WRITE side: a bare `.inf` is refused on the encrypt path,
because libyaml leaves the spelling a string while Go resolves it to float +Inf,
so the document would fail its own MAC (measured there, sops -d exit 51).

The READ side is not covered and fails. sops itself writes such a document:

  plaintext:  keep: x / v_unencrypted: .inf
  sops -e     exit 0, wire holds `.inf` verbatim
  sops -d     exit 0

  File::SOPS->decrypt of that same document, 3e5e6b0:
  MAC verification failed: the digest over 2 leaf values in document order
  does not match the one stored in the sops section.

Mechanism: YAML::XS returns `.inf` as a POK-ONLY scalar -- a plain string -- so
our digest covers the four characters `.inf`, while Go resolved a float and
digested `+Inf`. Same for .Inf .INF +.inf -.inf .nan .NaN .NAN (%GO_CONSTANT in
Format::YAML already lists all eight and the bytes Go derives from each).

Not fixable the way #102 is. #102 turns a leaf our parser wrongly made a FLOAT
back into the string Go sees; here our parser already has a string and Go has the
float, so the repair would have to go the other way -- manufacture a real Perl
+Inf on parse -- which assert_representable then refuses on any re-encrypt
(karr #59). So the read fix and the write refusal would contradict each other,
and that tension is the actual open question. Do not resolve it by loosening the
non-finite guard.

Reachability is why this is low: the document has to be written by sops (ours
refuses to create one) and has to carry a bare `.inf` in an UNENCRYPTED slot.
An encrypted `.inf` is fine and stays fine -- sops stores it as type:float with
the plaintext `+Inf`, and File::SOPS decrypts it to a real Perl Inf today
(measured, identical before and after #102's prototype).

Wire lane. Needs the sops binary to prove.
